# Session changes

Summary of fixes made while debugging the Maven build, then the MQTT-SN 2.0 gateway against
the `paho.mqtt-sn.embedded-c` `test22`/`test23` network test suites.

## mqtt-sn-core

- **`impl/AbstractTopicRegistry.java`** — `topicPath()` had `case FULL: break;` falling through
  without extracting the topic string, even though `FULL`-type `TopicInfo` carries it exactly
  like `SHORT` does. This made any MQTT-SN 2.0 client publish using a full topic name directly in
  `PUBLISH` (without a prior `REGISTER`) throw `MqttsnExpectationFailedException`, which the
  message handler swallowed into a generic `SERVER_UNAVAILABLE`/malformed ack — breaking QoS 1/2
  PUBACK/PUBREC and subscribe-then-echo tests. Fixed by adding `topicPath = topicInfo.getTopicPath();`
  for the `FULL` case.
- **`utils/Files.java`** — `createRuntimeLockFile()` only ever released its `.lck` marker via a
  JVM shutdown hook, never on an explicit service `stop()`. Added `releaseRuntimeLockFile()`.
- **`impl/MqttsnFilesystemStorageService.java`** — added a `stop()` override calling
  `Files.releaseRuntimeLockFile(path)`, so a runtime can be stopped and a new one started against
  the same workspace within the same JVM (this is exactly what `SubscriptionTests` does per
  `@Test` method) without hitting `workspace in use`.
- **`tree/MqttTree.java`** — single-level wildcard (`+`) subscriptions never matched when the `+`
  was the *last* segment of the filter (e.g. `sport/tennis/+` didn't match `sport/tennis/player1`,
  though `sport/+/player1` worked). `searchChildren()` recursed into nested wildcard subtrees but
  never checked whether the wildcard node itself was the terminal match. Fixed by copying the
  wildcard node's own members when there are no path segments left to consume.
- **`test/NoopTestTransport.java`** (new) — minimal no-op `IMqttsnTransport` so in-VM test
  runtimes have a registered transport (required to construct network contexts).
- **`test/MqttsnTestRuntimeRegistry.java`** — wired `NoopTestTransport` into
  `defaultConfiguration(...)`, since it never registered a transport before, causing
  `getDefaultTransport()` to throw `no transports available on runtime` for any test creating a
  session.

## mqtt-sn-protection-runtimes

- **`test/TestKeyWrite.java` → `test/KeyFileGenerator.java`** (renamed) — this was a one-off
  key-generation utility disguised as a JUnit `@Test`, with the original author's own machine's
  absolute path hardcoded (`/Users/simonjohnson/IdeaProjects/...`), so it failed
  `FileNotFoundException` on any other machine via `mvn test`. Renamed (removes it from Surefire's
  default `Test*.java` discovery pattern), dropped `@Test`/JUnit dependency, changed to a
  `public static void main`, and made the output path relative (`src/main/resources`) so it's
  still runnable manually if the key material needs regenerating.
- **`runtime/ProtectionExampleGatewayCli.java`** — registered an additional trusted sender using
  the exact raw Sender ID (`DEADBEEFCAFEBABE`) and HMAC-SHA256 key that `paho.mqtt-sn.embedded-c`'s
  `test3.c`/`test23` suite (and the C++ `MQTTSN2Gateway`'s `test-gateway.conf`) expect, alongside
  the existing `client1` demo sender — so one running instance now answers both.

## mqtt-sn-protection

- **`impl/InMemoryProtectedSenderRegistry.java`** — added `addAllowedRawSenderId(byte[], ProtectedSender)`.
  The existing `addAllowedClientId(...)` always derives the wire Sender ID via
  `hash(clientId)[0:N]`; there was no way to register a sender under a literal, pre-agreed raw
  byte sequence (needed for interop with peers that don't derive their ID from a clientId
  string, e.g. `test3.c`).
- **`impl/MqttsnProtectionService.java`** — `isSecurityEnvelope()` compared the incoming wire
  message type against `MqttsnConstants.PROTECTION` (`0x1E`), but the actual PROTECTION wire type
  used by `MqttsnProtection.getMessageType()` is `PROTECTION_ENCAPSULATION_V2_0` (`0xFF`). Since
  those never matched, every incoming PROTECTION-wrapped packet fell through to the plain
  (non-protection) integrity check and failed with "message integrity check failed", so the
  gateway never even attempted to unwrap protected traffic. Fixed the comparison to use the
  correct constant.

## mqtt-sn-codec

- **`MqttsnConstants.java`** — `FORWARDER_ENCAPSULATION_V2_0`, `SESSION_ENCAPSULATION_V2_0`, and
  `PROTECTION_ENCAPSULATION_V2_0` were declared as `byte` with value `0xFF`/`0xFE`/`0xFD`. Java
  sign-extends a `byte` to `int` on widening, so `(byte) 0xFF` becomes `-1`, not `255` — meaning
  `case MqttsnConstants.PROTECTION_ENCAPSULATION_V2_0:` in `Mqttsn_v2_0_Codec`'s `switch(int)`
  dispatch could never match the unsigned `255` produced by `MqttsnWireUtils.readMessageType()`.
  Every PROTECTION packet fell through to the `default` case, which delegates to the v1.2
  dispatcher, throwing `MqttsnCodecException: unknown message type [255]`. Changed all three
  constants to `int`, matching the convention already used for the `RETURN_CODE_*_V2_0` block
  (which is `int`-typed for exactly this reason).
  - Note: this required a `mvn clean install` (not just `install`) to take effect, since javac
    inlines `static final` constants at compile time — files that only *reference* the constant
    (like `Mqttsn_v2_0_Codec.java`) aren't recompiled by Maven's incremental compiler just because
    the constant's own source file changed.

## paho.mqtt-sn.embedded-c

- **`MQTTSN2Packet/test/test3.c`** — `TEST_TOPIC_LEN` was `18` for `"test/mqttsn2-prot"`, which is
  17 characters; the extra byte put a trailing NUL into the wire-encoded topic, which the gateway
  correctly rejected as an invalid topic (`RETURN_CODE_INVALID_TOPIC_ID`), failing the SUBSCRIBE
  sub-test. Fixed `TEST_TOPIC_LEN` to `17`.

## Result

- `mvn clean install` passes for every module in `mqtt-sn` except `mqtt-sn-cloud-client`, whose
  tests require live internet access to an external discovery service unavailable in this
  sandbox (unrelated to any of the above).
- `test22` (plain MQTT-SN 2.0 network suite) passes all 10 sub-tests against
  `mqtt-sn-gateway-console` (run with `-DprotocolVersion=2`).
- `test23` (MQTT-SN 2.0 PROTECTION suite) passes all 9 sub-tests against
  `ProtectionExampleGatewayCli`.
